@@ -0,0 +1,98 @@
+# WD12 stopped — application input and effect oracle contract is missing
+
+Base: accepted WD11 `91a3cda9993d900381502719c86be99ee8eac53b`. This is a bounded feasibility
+review and offline counterexample, not an implemented drill or passing WD12 Acceptance.
+
+## Concrete Acceptance blocker
+
+WD12 requires a design-derived ordinary successful request after recovery and, for every
+consumer, a valid message delivered twice with one recorded product effect. It restricts the
+drill to existing operator health/contract/receipt/progress surfaces. Those surfaces do not
+provide application-valid inputs or identify/assert the application effect.
+
+- `src/v2/deployment.ts` describes processes, stores, uses, calls and HTTP health. It contains
+  no sample requests/messages, authentication setup or product-effect oracle. The fresh exports'
+  actual field inventories are in `export-audit.json`; normalized stub declarations specify
+  route/loop/library roles and resource links, not test inputs or effect expectations.
+- Catalog's application-owned `catalogItem` accepts a particular id/title shape. Its receipt
+  definition has `database`, delivery horizon, retention, `map` and `complete` only. Its
+  `products.json` registers `apply_catalog` as a three-parameter command on deliveries. It
+  supplies no product query/read route for observing that command's business effect.
+- The RG23 harness supplies the missing facts: `rg-catalog.ts`'s `publish` chooses the valid
+  `{id:'video-one',title:'Catalog video',date:...}` payload; its `effects` observer selects the
+  product-specific `effects` column from `p_caller_owner.deliveries`. `send` and `webhook` also
+  choose fixture-specific ordinary requests and authentication/signing input. Transport,
+  bounded polling, exact ownership/cleanup and native observation helpers can be copied.
+  Copying these fixture policies into generic generation would hardcode product behavior.
+- The existing extension API, generated extension-writing packet, `.slots.ts`, settings and
+  product SQL registration have no sample/observer/test registration. Application stubs accept
+  product logic, but do not define a drill input/expectation interface.
+
+A possible continuation is an application-owned test driver/configuration containing request,
+authentication and message factories plus effect assertions through ordinary operator SQL or
+application routes. That introduces a mandatory test/oracle contract and handwritten
+responsibility beyond the approved operator-only design-derived drill. The blocker is that
+interface decision, not the mere addition of a helper file, nor implementation complexity.
+No such interface was introduced. Choosing receipt/progress as a substitute would weaken the
+required one-product-effect observation.
+
+## Executed counterexample
+
+The public CLI freshly exported `rg-link-flow` and `rg-catalog-flow`: 113 and 97 files including
+their manifests, zero findings, with the normal 20 and 7 explicit application gaps. Their
+original attachment manifests supplied application-owned code. Only catalog's application-owned
+`apply-catalog.ts` was replaced by the new `wd-drill-no-effect` fixture variant. Its unchanged
+mapping validates the actual catalog DTO; its `complete` callback validates then returns
+without issuing a product command. Every generated scaffold remains byte-identical to its
+manifest (85 link / 74 catalog scaffolds). No generated implementation was manually edited.
+
+Both actual exported API8 checkers pass, including the deliberately defective callback. This
+is expected: cooperative boundary checks do not establish product completeness or behavior.
+No emitter changed, no pin was refreshed, and a full generated TypeScript build was not run.
+
+`wd12-drill-feasibility.test.ts` loads the actual freshly exported receipt runtime and variant
+via Node's type stripping, sharing the repository's existing operation/transaction test
+loader and PGlite helper. The actual catalog product migration creates deliveries in PGlite.
+It observes:
+
+1. Generic `{}` fails with `MALFORMED_MESSAGE` under the application mapper.
+2. The application's valid id/title payload succeeds twice under one stable message ID.
+3. The generated receipt store contains one receipt with `result: {value:true}`.
+4. Generated consumer progress says running and progressed.
+5. The actual catalog deliveries table contains zero rows and zero summed effects.
+
+`counterexample.json` and its successful Vitest log retain these observations. The initial
+focused run also passed using the helper's generic empty product table; a targeted rerun after
+switching the observer to the actual catalog schema/migration is the recorded final proof.
+This direct offline invocation does not exercise broker transport, two OS replicas, stop/start,
+native recovery, a bad-readiness variant or either required exported drill. None is claimed.
+
+## Feasible implementation details kept separate
+
+Loop-only workers have no HTTP health listener. The actual lifecycle maintains
+`/tmp/application-ready`, their container/PID indicates continued process liveness, and generated
+receipt worker state distinguishes heartbeat/progress. RG23 already observes these surfaces.
+Using them instead of inventing worker `/readyz` routes is a nearest-detail implementation
+choice, not the stopping reason.
+
+Optional positive caches correctly stay ready and fall back to their original source. Necessary
+database/admission/broker loss removes readiness. Accepted WD11 native evidence proves those
+different behaviors; a blanket cache-unready expectation would weaken the RG contract.
+The fresh link design contains databases/caches and no broker. It needs no broker scenario.
+No change to these guarantees, no test-control route, dependency or production capability was
+made. No container was created or stopped for this offline investigation.
+
+## Reproduction before restoration
+
+From the base checkout, apply the stopped patch and use Node 24.21.0. Run the two public CLI
+commands recorded in `export-{link,catalog}.log`, with empty `/tmp/jeeves-wd12/{link,catalog}-export`
+destinations, then run `prepare-exports.py`. Link each export's `node_modules` to the repository's
+existing dependencies and run its own `scripts/check-extensions.mjs <export-root>`. Run:
+
+```sh
+pnpm exec vitest run --config tooling/vitest.config.ts jeeves-generator/src/v2/wd12-drill-feasibility.test.ts
+```
+
+This needs existing dependencies, no install, network, Docker or PostgreSQL. New fixture input,
+test, review scripts, receipts and fresh-export evidence are retained only inside `WD12.patch`.
+The restored-tree regression and final preservation results live in the append-only handoff.
